---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Case Study: Kubernetes Pod Scheduling Failure | InfraOpsX"
  description="An anonymized Kubernetes case study showing how scheduler events exposed affinity and CPU constraints behind a Pending Pod."
  lang="en"
  canonicalPath="/case-studies/kubernetes-pod-pending/"
  enPath="/case-studies/kubernetes-pod-pending/"
  zhPath="/zh/case-studies/kubernetes-pod-pending/"
>
  <Header
    locale="en"
    switchHref="/zh/case-studies/kubernetes-pod-pending/"
  />

  <main class="case-detail-shell">
    <div class="container case-detail-container">

      <div class="article-breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <a href="/case-studies/">Case Studies</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span>Kubernetes Scheduling Failure</span>
      </div>

      <header class="case-detail-header">
        <span class="section-label">CASE STUDY · KUBERNETES</span>

        <h1>
          Diagnosing a Kubernetes Pod that could not be scheduled
        </h1>

        <p class="case-lead">
          A workload remained in <code>Pending</code>. Instead of treating it as
          a generic Kubernetes failure, scheduler events were used to turn the
          problem into a small set of concrete scheduling constraints.
        </p>

        <div class="case-facts">
          <div>
            <span>Environment</span>
            <strong>6-node Kubernetes cluster</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Primary signal</span>
            <strong>Scheduler Events</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Areas involved</span>
            <strong>Affinity · CPU requests</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span>Outcome</span>
            <strong>Scheduling blockers identified</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <article class="case-detail-content">

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">01 · Problem</span>
          <h2>The visible symptom was only “Pending”</h2>

          <p>
            A Pod stayed in <code>Pending</code> and was not assigned to any
            node. At that point the status itself did not explain whether the
            cause was CPU, memory, affinity, taints, storage or another
            scheduling restriction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repeatedly deleting the Pod would simply recreate the same
            specification and hit the same constraints again, so the first goal
            was to read the scheduler's own evidence.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">02 · Evidence</span>
          <h2>The scheduler had already explained the failure</h2>

          <p>The key command was:</p>

          <pre><code>kubectl describe pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre>

          <p>The Events section reported:</p>

          <pre><code>0/6 nodes are available:
2 node(s) didn't match pod affinity rules,
4 Insufficient cpu.

preemption:
0/6 nodes are available:
2 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling,
4 No preemption victims found for incoming pod.</code></pre>

          <p>
            That message reduced the investigation dramatically. There were six
            possible nodes, but every one of them was excluded by a known
            constraint.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">03 · Diagnosis</span>
          <h2>Translate the event into a constraint table</h2>

          <div class="case-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Constraint</th>
                  <th>Nodes excluded</th>
                  <th>Meaning</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Affinity mismatch</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                  <td>The workload's placement rules rejected those nodes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Insufficient CPU</td>
                  <td>4</td>
                  <td>The remaining nodes could not satisfy the Pod's CPU request.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Remaining candidates</td>
                  <td>0</td>
                  <td>No node satisfied all constraints at the same time.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The important distinction is that Kubernetes scheduling uses
            resource <strong>requests</strong>, not just live utilization.
            A node can look lightly loaded in <code>top</code> or
            <code>kubectl top</code> and still be unavailable because its
            allocatable CPU is already reserved by existing requests.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">04 · Validation path</span>
          <h2>Check the two blocking dimensions independently</h2>

          <h3>CPU reservation</h3>

          <pre><code>kubectl describe pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; &lt;pod-name&gt;
kubectl describe node &lt;node-name&gt;
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods -A</code></pre>

          <p>
            The relevant values are the Pod's CPU request, each node's
            allocatable resources, and the resources already reserved by other
            workloads.
          </p>

          <h3>Affinity rules</h3>

          <pre><code>kubectl get pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; &lt;pod-name&gt; -o yaml
kubectl get nodes --show-labels</code></pre>

          <p>
            The goal is not to remove affinity blindly. The rule may exist for
            availability, topology or workload isolation. The correct question
            is whether the current rule still matches the intended deployment
            design.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">05 · Preemption</span>
          <h2>“Preemption is not helpful” was another useful clue</h2>

          <p>
            The scheduler also evaluated whether evicting lower-priority Pods
            could create a valid placement and concluded that it would not.
          </p>

          <p>
            This makes sense: evicting another Pod cannot fix an affinity
            mismatch. It also cannot help if there is no suitable victim whose
            removal creates enough allocatable CPU on an otherwise valid node.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">06 · Corrective options</span>
          <h2>The fix depends on the original design intent</h2>

          <p>
            Once the constraints were clear, the possible corrective actions
            were also clear. The right choice depends on the workload and
            cluster design:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Correct an affinity rule that is stricter than intended.</li>
            <li>Reduce an unnecessarily high CPU request.</li>
            <li>Move or resize other workloads.</li>
            <li>Add cluster capacity.</li>
            <li>Adjust node labels or topology where appropriate.</li>
          </ul>

          <blockquote>
            The case study intentionally stops at the verified diagnosis rather
            than claiming a production change that was not independently
            documented.
          </blockquote>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">07 · Result</span>
          <h2>The problem was no longer “Kubernetes is broken”</h2>

          <p>
            The useful outcome of the investigation was a precise explanation:
            two nodes were disqualified by affinity and the other four could
            not satisfy CPU requests. There were zero valid scheduling
            candidates.
          </p>

          <p>
            That changed the next step from trial-and-error into an
            architecture decision: adjust placement policy, resource requests,
            workload distribution or cluster capacity.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span class="case-step">08 · Takeaway</span>
          <h2>Diagnose first. Change second.</h2>

          <p>
            For an unscheduled Pod, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> is often
            the fastest way to turn a vague <code>Pending</code> state into a
            concrete list of constraints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scheduler events are not background noise. In many cases, they are
            already the shortest path to the root cause.
          </p>
        </section>

      </article>

      <div class="case-detail-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/case-studies/">← All case studies</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/blog/kubernetes-pod-pending/">
          Read the troubleshooting guide
        </a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer locale="en" />
</BaseLayout>
